5:[["$","$L10","bnss",{"law":{"id":"bnss","title":"Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023","short":"BNSS","year":"2023 · in force 1 July 2024","kind":"Act","status":"current","summary":"Criminal procedure: information, investigation, production, search, seizure, electronic processes, and trial.","url":"https://www.mha.gov.in/sites/default/files/250884_2_english_01042024.pdf","caution":"Use the applicable version and state notifications. Electronic processes do not remove authentication, signature, or other safeguards.","topics":["2.3","2.2","1.7"],"sections":[{"number":"94","title":"Summoning production of documents or things","text":"Broadly succeeds CrPC section 91. A qualifying authority may require production of a necessary or desirable document or thing, including electronic records, subject to the provision and exclusions."},{"number":"105","title":"Audio-video recording of search and seizure","text":"Provides mandatory recording for specified searches using mobile phone or other electronic means, with the stated progression toward implementation. Check the current notification and read with section 185."},{"number":"106","title":"Attachment of property from criminal activity","text":"Provides a mechanism concerning proceeds of crime in specified circumstances, with approval and notice requirements. It is not a general shortcut to confiscation without procedure."},{"number":"173","title":"Information in cognizable cases","text":"Broadly succeeds CrPC 154 and expressly addresses electronic communication, signature within the specified period, and information by a woman for specified offences. Subsection (3) adds a preliminary-enquiry mechanism for certain offences and conditions."},{"number":"174","title":"Investigation of cognizable cases","text":"Addresses investigation of cognizable cases and specified forensic procedures for serious offences, including audio-video documentation. Establish applicability and practical implementation."},{"number":"180","title":"Examination of witnesses","text":"Broadly succeeds CrPC 161. Record the examination accurately and follow the signature, recording, and other safeguards in the applicable provision."},{"number":"183","title":"Confessions and statements","text":"Broadly succeeds CrPC 164. The magistrate, recording method, warnings, signature, and forwarding requirements must be followed. A forensic examiner does not acquire this power merely by expertise."},{"number":"185","title":"Search by police officer","text":"Broadly succeeds CrPC 165, with expanded recording and audiovisual-search provisions. Record grounds, scope, things sought, and execution in accordance with the current text."},{"number":"193","title":"Police report","text":"Broadly succeeds the investigation-report function of CrPC 173. Track the statutory report, further investigation, and specified communication duties using the current section numbering."},{"number":"528","title":"Inherent powers of the High Court","text":"Broadly corresponds to CrPC 482. It preserves specified inherent powers to prevent abuse of process or secure the ends of justice, subject to legal limits."},{"number":"530","title":"Electronic mode of proceedings","text":"Provides for specified trials, inquiries, and proceedings by electronic means. Implementation, rules, and the nature of the proceeding must be checked; it is not an unconditional virtual-trial entitlement."},{"number":"534","title":"Repeal and savings","text":"Contains the transition from the CrPC. Determine how pending investigations, proceedings, and earlier actions are saved rather than applying a simple date-only rule to every procedural question."}]}}]]
